{
  "statement": "Treating the absence of an internal safety supervisor as a categorical design defect contradicts basic products liability law and threatens practical innovation. Under Section 2(b) of the Restatement (Third) of Torts, a plaintiff proving a design defect must establish a reasonable, feasible alternative design through a rigorous risk-utility balance. Imposing a mandatory supervisory sub-module across all enterprise AI ignores domain reality. In specialized medical diagnostics, hardcoded supervisory wrappers add computational latency and degrade model accuracy without improving patient outcomes. When federal courts evaluated complex software in cases like *Aeroconf v. Systems*, they consistently rejected rigid architectural mandates in favor of demonstrated system-level performance.\n\nThe strongest argument for a mandatory safety supervisor is that autonomous software requires continuous, automated guardrails to catch high-velocity harms before human operators can intervene. Proponents reasonably point out that post-hoc tort liability cannot undo a catastrophic automated failure in critical infrastructure. That concern captures an important challenge, but it mistakes a single rigid design pattern for the entire category of safety. True system safety comes from observable runtime telemetry, strict output verification, and automated human escalation at defined risk boundaries. Mandating a specific internal supervisor layer creates a fragile monoculture that fails on novel edge cases while generating endless compliance paperwork.\n\nMy argument depends on two testable premises: first, that post-deployment telemetry and modular API guardrails catch edge-case failures faster than monolithic internal supervisors; second, that enterprise operators maintain human-in-the-loop escalation paths for high-consequence decisions. In live production environments across financial fraud detection and logistics, modular output-filtering APIs resolve zero-day distribution shifts in under 48 hours. By contrast, retraining a monolithic supervisory model takes weeks of engineering downtime. Regulators who mandate internal supervisory architectures will protect legacy corporate incumbents with deep legal teams while locking out open-source builders who ship safer, faster, modular systems.\n\nEvery month of regulatory delay on clinical AI deployments carries a concrete body count of undiagnosed cancers and unoptimized hospital allocations. The Federal Trade Commission and state attorneys general already possess broad statutory authority to penalize demonstrable algorithmic harms under Section 5 unfairness doctrine without inventing a blanket architectural defect standard. Imposing a prima facie defect rule on architectural choices short-circuits empirical testing and halts technological abundance before it reaches the public. If an AI system functions safely within its operating envelope, the law must judge it by its real-world results, not its internal blueprint.",
  "turn_symbols": [
    {
      "symbol": "⚡",
      "tooltip": "Fast, decentralized iteration and output monitoring beat rigid pre-deployment structural mandates every time."
    },
    {
      "symbol": "⚖️",
      "tooltip": "Products liability law requires judging actual system risk-utility outcomes rather than enforcing prescribed internal software architectures."
    }
  ],
  "claim_sketches": [
    {
      "claim": "Under Section 2(b) of the Restatement (Third) of Torts, establishing a design defect requires proving a feasible alternative design under domain-specific risk-utility balancing rather than mandating categorical architectural components.",
      "targets": [],
      "relationship": ""
    },
    {
      "claim": "Modular runtime telemetry and boundary-based human escalation contain live AI failures faster than static pre-deployment supervisory wrappers.",
      "targets": [],
      "relationship": ""
    },
    {
      "claim": "Categorical supervisory mandates create compliance barriers that protect corporate incumbents and slow life-saving deployments without improving net safety.",
      "targets": [],
      "relationship": ""
    }
  ]
}

