{
  "statement": "Autonomous foundation models cannot claim raw material immunity under Section 5 of the Restatement (Third) of Torts. The strongest version of the opposing argument is that upstream developers build general-purpose software components without knowing where downstream developers will deploy them. Under classic tort law, a supplier of raw steel does not bear liability when a contractor builds a defective bridge. That legal shield fails here because a foundation model with autonomous tool-execution capabilities is not an inert raw material. It is an active mechanical subassembly that generates unpredictable actions. When developers ship foundation models without standardized telemetry hooks or execution boundaries, they release a product with an inherent design defect under established products liability doctrine.\n\nThe law must establish a bifurcated duty of care across the artificial intelligence supply chain. Upstream foundation developers must deliver verified telemetry interfaces and state-space boundaries, while downstream deployers bear the non-delegable duty to activate deterministic runtime circuit breakers before granting external write permissions. An artificial intelligence system's real-world blast radius scales directly with its access permissions to enterprise databases and financial rails. As documented in \"The Rush to Adopt AI: Business Risks & How to Get it Right,\" autonomous tools routinely require broad access to corporate repositories like Salesforce, Slack, and Google Workspace. A six-hour exploit window in unmonitored systems can wipe out enterprise data before human supervisors notice the breach. Upstream providers must engineer the emergency brake, and downstream operators must keep a foot hovering over the pedal.\n\nFailing to mandate runtime circuit breakers guarantees catastrophic fault fragmentation that destroys startups while shielding platform monopolies. Over 5,000 artificial intelligence startups launched between 2013 and 2023 operate on razor-thin margins. Meanwhile, massive infrastructure alliances like the NVIDIA and OpenAI compute partnerships detailed in \"2026 Antitrust Year in Preview  AI   Wilson Sonsini\" capture upstream profits. If downstream startups bear sole enterprise liability when autonomous agents fail, a single systemic exploit will liquidate those small companies. Injured consumers will recover nothing from bankrupt startups while upstream platforms walk away with toll revenue. Standardized out-of-band circuit breakers protect small deployers by establishing an insurable, bounded operating perimeter that caps tail liabilities before deployment.\n\nCongress and state attorneys general can enforce this framework through existing product safety mechanisms. The National Highway Traffic Safety Administration already mandates standard braking assemblies on automotive chassis before manufacturers sell them to commercial upfitters. State attorneys general, armed with statutes like Colorado SB 24-205, can penalize any enterprise deploying unmonitored agentic systems in high-risk environments. This framework relies on two clear assumptions: deterministic circuit breakers can halt execution out-of-band without degrading model throughput, and regulators can define high-risk write permissions with objective technical thresholds. A bipartisan coalition of state regulators, consumer protection groups, and commercial insurers will back this standard because it prices risk transparently. If policymakers permit upstream vendors to sell unmonitored agentic tools without kill switches, the public will bear the ruinous costs of the inevitable failures.",
  "turn_symbols": [
    {
      "symbol": "🛑",
      "tooltip": "Mandatory out-of-band circuit breakers act as non-negotiable emergency brakes on autonomous system permissions."
    },
    {
      "symbol": "⚖️",
      "tooltip": "A bifurcated duty of care balances safety burdens fairly between upstream foundation developers and downstream runtime deployers."
    }
  ],
  "claim_sketches": [
    {
      "claim": "Autonomous foundation models cannot claim raw material immunity under Section 5 of the Restatement (Third) of Torts.",
      "targets": ["AN-32"]
    },
    {
      "claim": "The law must establish a bifurcated duty of care across the artificial intelligence supply chain.",
      "targets": ["AN-32", "AN-33"]
    },
    {
      "claim": "Failing to mandate runtime circuit breakers guarantees catastrophic fault fragmentation that destroys startups while shielding platform monopolies.",
      "targets": ["AN-30", "AN-32"]
    },
    {
      "claim": "Congress and state attorneys general can enforce this framework through existing product safety mechanisms.",
      "targets": ["AN-32", "AN-33"]
    }
  ]
}

