  "statement": "Mandating commercial liability insurance without dynamic telemetry will kill early-stage competition while leaving tail risks unpriced. Skeptic correctly argues that enterprise balance sheets should absorb the financial fallout of deployment failures rather than hiding behind compliance checklists. But private underwriters cannot price statistical model drift in the dark. In traditional lines like commercial aviation, underwriters rely on standardized flight data recorders with decades of baseline actuarial history. For open-ended neural models, that historical actuarial baseline does not exist. A blanket insurance mandate today simply forces underwriters to charge massive capital reserves, pricing out the more than 5,000 AI startups founded across the United States over the past decade. It hands the entire AI ecosystem to a handful of well-capitalized tech incumbents.\n\nAutomated runtime supervisor logs will not solve this underwriting vacuum. Safetyist believes that out-of-band monitoring traces create clear audit trails that hold executives liable for operational harms. In practice, high-dimensional neural activity produces an expert-inspectability gap. Billions of internal weight updates and activation vectors create millions of log entries that forensic examiners cannot decipher after an incident. This volume allows corporate defense attorneys to bury regulators in petabytes of opaque telemetry. The Texas Responsible Artificial Intelligence Governance Act addressed this problem by coupling a 36-month regulatory sandbox with a 60-day opportunity to cure identified operational failures. Real accountability comes from standardizing observable system performance and input-output boundaries, not from requiring undecipherable internal logs.\n\nThe sensible policy mechanism is telemetry-underwritten liability anchored strictly at the downstream deployment layer. Upstream base model developers cannot predict every end-use context, and downstream application developers lack access to internal base weights. Placing strict architectural supervisor mandates on base weights misassigns legal duty. Instead, regulators should let insurers underwrite policies based on standardized real-time telemetry from live operational wrappers. Underwriters can then adjust coverage dynamically as downstream deployers demonstrate stability in production. This market approach aligns corporate incentives, keeps open-source foundation models viable, and ensures injured parties collect real payouts from solvent, insured deployers.",
