{
  "statement": "Downstream developers integrate proprietary models through closed commercial APIs without access to internal model weights. That technical reality is undisputed, and it represents a genuine engineering bottleneck for small application developers who cannot inspect the models they license. But treating this opacity as an unfixable technical barrier ignores how corporate balance sheets work. Upstream tech giants deliberately structure their API terms to extract toll revenue while pushing tort liability onto undercapitalized startups. More than 5,000 AI startups launched in the United States between 2013 and 2023, and most of them operate on thin margins while three cloud providers capture the computing profits.\n\nMandating deterministic runtime supervisors under Section 2(b) of the Restatement (Third) of Torts will not fix this structural imbalance. In practice, technical supervisor rules become check-the-box compliance shields for the largest providers. A tech giant can run an out-of-band monitoring filter, log its operational telemetry, and claim immunity under component-part doctrine when downstream failures occur. That procedural safe harbor leaves injured victims with zero recourse against the well-funded entity that built the underlying engine. Underwriters do not need mandatory architectural checkboxes to calculate risk. Insurers need solvent corporate defendants who cannot hide behind multi-tier software licensing agreements.\n\nCourts and regulators must apply strict enterprise liability directly to base model developers and compute platforms. When a hospital deployment discriminates against patients or a financial model denies credit illegally, civil litigants should obtain broad discovery into training datasets and internal corporate risk assessments across the entire supply chain. Congress already introduced the AI Civil Rights Act to target discriminatory outcomes in housing and credit, proving that public policy can focus on material harms rather than software sub-modules. If upstream platform monopolies face joint-and-several liability when their commercial engines cause physical, financial, or civil rights damage, they will build rigorous safety controls without waiting for a regulatory mandate.\n\nWho profits from selling closed API access while externalizing the cost of catastrophic failure onto the public and small developers?",
