  "statement": "The Skeptic argues that upstream cloud providers will exploit Section 5 of the Restatement (Third) of Torts. They argue that tech giants will deliver basic telemetry hooks, sign boilerplate indemnities, and leave undercapitalized startups holding all legal liability. That concern identifies a real corporate tactic. Yet established products liability law forbids manufacturers from delegating strict liability for defective designs through private contracts. As analyzed in \"The Law of AI is the Law of Risky Agents Without Intentions\", objective legal standards must bind human principals who profit from risky automated systems. When lawmakers drafted the Financial Artificial Intelligence Risk Reduction Act in December 2023, they recognized that strict liability must replace intent requirements to prevent corporate evasion.\n\nUnder Section 5 of the Restatement, component sellers face direct liability when a component is inherently defective or when the manufacturer substantially participates in downstream integration. Upstream foundation models are not inert raw materials like steel or cement. Hyperscalers train autonomous capabilities, build execution tooling, and retain continuous API control over runtime compute. When a model exhibits predictable failure modes during automated tool execution, that failure constitutes an upstream design defect. Federal courts do not permit aircraft engine manufacturers to disclaim liability when a foreseeable engine flaw downs an airliner. Upstream cloud providers cannot contract out of strict liability when their core architecture generates systemic harm.\n\nStatic regulatory sandboxes cannot cure these structural risks. The Accelerationist points to the 36-month sandbox under Texas House Bill 149 as a model for safe innovation. That approach assumes AI models maintain constant performance over time. As documented in the NIST \"AI RMF Playbook\", dynamic AI systems silently degrade as operational conditions shift after deployment. A multi-year sandbox creates an exculpatory shelter while runtime drift introduces unmonitored hazards into enterprise workflows. Safety requires continuous runtime circuit breakers and real-time execution bounds, not temporary regulatory waivers that expire only after unrecoverable damage occurs.\n\nEnforcing joint enterprise liability shifts the economic burden from injured consumers onto the hyperscale platforms best equipped to absorb and mitigate risk. This framework requires an active safety supervisor function with tamper-evident audit logging and mandatory liability insurance. This argument assumes courts will classify autonomous agent models as commercial products rather than protected speech. State attorneys general and the Federal Trade Commission possess existing statutory authority under unfair trade practice laws to police defective software deployments. Bipartisan coalitions of state regulators and consumer protection advocates support closing component liability loopholes. If lawmakers exempt upstream platforms from strict design defect liability, society will absorb the catastrophic financial and physical costs of unmonitored autonomous failure.",
